 (:adr/body "Owner instruction, 2026-09-09: kotoba only, and move further toward the pure
S-expression syntax; continue.

## The blocker was the toolchain, not the language

The pure S-expression core (`lam app ref perform rel query handle`, ADR-544)
is implemented in kotoba-sema and pinned by amu's main. The `kotoba` CLI
refused all of it, and the reason was three plies deep:

  * the SHARED amu checkout was 200 commits behind its own west pin. The pin
    was already correct (`24516340`); the tree was not.
  * that checkout pins kotoba-sema at `bf01d4a8`, 71 commits behind, which has
    no pure heads. amu's main pins `96fd4e19`, which does.
  * so the CLI everyone runs -- `~/.local/bin/kotoba` -> that checkout's
    bin/kotoba -- answered \"operation has no admitted lowering\" for a language
    feature that had been landed upstream for days.

This is the shape CLAUDE.md already names for library pins (\"a fix in main does
not arrive until someone names the newer sha\"), one level further out: the
CHECKOUT, not the pin, was the stale plane. `checkout / west pin / repo main`
are three different things and this was the third one.

Syncing the checkout found two more:

  * `node_modules` is committed on amu main as a SYMLINK TO ITS OWN ABSOLUTE
    PATH (25591e0a, 2026-09-06). In a checkout at that path it resolves to
    itself, and `bin/kotoba`'s shebang -- `npx --no-install nbb` -- dies with
    ELOOP, so the CLI does not start at all. `.gitignore` had `/node_modules/`;
    a trailing slash matches a directory and not a symlink. kotoba-lang/amu#907
    removes it (open: amu's main is protected by a GitHub Actions check, which
    is worth noting in a workspace whose CI is the murakumo fleet).
  * amu needs `npm install` for its own nbb; the tracked symlink had been
    standing in for it.

Measured after the sync: `(app (ref f) n)`, `(lam [x] ...)` and nested pure
application compile and RUN correctly. `(def f (lam [x] ...))` is NOT admitted
-- `def` takes constants -- so top-level definitions remain `defn` and the
pure core reaches call sites, lambdas and control, not definition shape.

## kotoba only: the compiler moved into Kotoba

`kotoba-lang/pattern` gains `kotoba/pattern_compile.kotoba`: the regex-subset
compiler, in Kotoba, in the pure core style -- `(app (ref f) x)` at call sites,
`if` and `let` for control, none of `cond`/`and`/`or`/threading. The `.cljc`
stays as the parity ORACLE rather than the implementation, so the library has
no Clojure host in its path.

Both compilers were run over every unique regex literal scraped from the
browser stack (200) and their programs compared instruction for instruction:

  identical programs 140   both refused 17   over a document bound 43
  SEMANTIC DISAGREEMENTS 0

Broken on purpose once: making `+` emit like `*` turned 46 into disagreements.

## The ceiling this port found, which is the real result

A `:document` is bounded at DEPTH 8, 256 NODES and 32 ITEMS PER CONTAINER
(`osaho/src/kotoba/kir/value.cljc`). The port carries its AST and its program
as documents, so 43 of the 200 patterns exceed one of those -- 32 depth, 10
vector size, 1 node count -- and none of them disagrees about meaning.

The consequence is bigger than this library: A PROGRAM OF MORE THAN 32
INSTRUCTIONS CANNOT BE A GUEST DOCUMENT AT ALL. And there is nothing to escape
into: `[:vector T]` is a bounded heterogeneous TUPLE type (\"heterogeneous
vector types must be a bounded vector\"), and no backend has a sequence
parameter type. The way out is to carry the program as a `:string` -- the value
limit is 65536 bytes -- and index into it, which is about 300x and is the next
slice.

`test/compile_parity.cljs` holds 43 as a RATCHET: red on a semantic
disagreement, and red if the count moves in either direction.

## Three more measured facts

  * `document-merge` is for MAPS. Handed two vectors it fails the :ir lowering
    with `document-map-required`, AFTER `check` and the subset pass accepted
    the module. There is no vector-concatenation head, so the port carries one.
  * `kotoba -M test`'s fuel budget is fixed and `--fuel` does not reach it:
    five of the compiler's eight tests trap on :js and :wasm while all eight
    pass on :jvm-kir. Fuel is charged per function ENTRY, so a module written
    as many small pure helpers -- which is what the pure core encourages --
    spends proportionally more of it.
  * `pattern_core`'s matcher is refused by the KIR evaluator: every one of its
    tests answers \"value is not a boolean\" on :jvm-kir while the JS artifact
    answers correctly and agrees with the host's RegExp 216/216. Seven targeted
    probes -- option bool, record bool field, document-map bool,
    `document-edn-read`, `string-code-point-at`, a self-call inside its own
    argument, forward mutual recursion -- all pass on all three targets, so it
    is not any of those alone and has not been reduced further.

## What this is not

The 43 are not fixed, only measured and ratcheted. The KIR refusal is recorded,
not diagnosed. amu#907 is open, not merged. And no consumer has been rewritten
to use the pattern library yet: the cheapest first is `kiyaku.account`'s six
postal formats, all of which the subset admits and all of which fit inside the
document bounds.
")))
